#!/bin/sh
# script/soak-test: run the sustained multicore workload under QEMU and judge it the way a board is
# judged (milestone 219).
#
#   script/soak-test                             # aarch64, one minute
#   script/soak-test --arch riscv64 --for 10m    # the architecture radon runs
#   script/soak-test --arch x86_64 --smp 1       # xenon's architecture, forced to one core
#   script/soak-test --reboot --arch x86_64      # milestone 249 (the boot lottery is sampled by a person walking to the board): reset, and prove it came back
#
# `--reboot` builds `--features reboot_soak_test` instead, and passes only when the soak's start line
# appears a second time after the kernel asked for a cold reboot: QEMU reset the machine and loaded
# the kernel again. It is the per-architecture proof of `arch::reboot` (SBI SRST, PSCI SYSTEM_RESET,
# the FADT reset register) and takes a little over two minutes, because the window is the board's
# own 120s. Exit 0 came back; 1 the reset was refused or the kernel panicked; 2 the deadline (six
# minutes unless `--for` says otherwise) passed; 3 QEMU's output ended, which is what a QEMU that
# exits on reset looks like and is not a reboot.
#
#   script/soak-test --wedge --arch x86_64       # milestone 593 (a wedged kernel resets itself): the TCO resets a wedge
#   script/soak-test --watchdog --arch x86_64    # milestone 593: a healthy one never is, over 180s
#   script/soak-test --no-watchdog --arch x86_64 # milestone 593: the plain soak with nothing to reset it
#
# **Every x86_64 soak arms the watchdog**, the plain one included (calef, 2026-09-26 UTC: "A soak
# should reset itself when it freezes"); kernel/build.rs decides it, so `cargo xtask uefi-image
# --features soak_test` gets it too. `--no-watchdog` builds `--features unwatched_soak_test`, the
# opt-out, for a run where a wedge must stay a wedge on the console instead of becoming a reset.
# Every riscv64 soak arms the JH7110's the same way. No QEMU board models it, so under `virt` the
# soak prints `NOT ARMED` and beats on; radon's bench step in milestone 593 is its proof. The
# aarch64 soak arms none.
#
# All three are x86_64 only, because the watchdog is the Intel TCO that q35's ICH9 emulates. `--wedge`
# builds `--features wedge_soak_test`, which stops beating 30s in, and passes when the start line
# appears again AND the second boot reports SECOND_TO_STS set, the chipset's record that its own
# second timeout reset the machine. `--watchdog` builds `--features watchdog_soak_test` and passes
# when the watchdog armed, was seen counting, and nothing reset the machine for `--for` (default
# 180s) after it armed. Exit statuses as `--reboot`'s. See kernel/src/arch/x86_64/tco.rs and
# design/roadmap/0593-a-wedged-kernel-resets-itself.md (number provisional).
#
# `design/fatal-risks.md`'s fifth entry ("it cannot be made reliable on multicore, and the bugs
# appear only on silicon") names its decisive experiment as sustained multi-core stress with the
# load-sensitive assertions live. This is that experiment's rehearsal: the same kernel feature, the
# same workload and the same judgement as a bench run, on a machine that cannot show the defects.
# The real runs happen on radon, argon and xenon; this is what proves the mechanism before anyone
# carries a board to a desk. See notes/soak.md.
#
# What it boots is `--features soak_test`, where the boot tour does not end in `arch::halt()` but in a
# pool of user-mode IPC workers and a supervisor that prints a heartbeat every five seconds. See
# kernel/src/soak.rs and fixtures/src/soaker.rs.
#
# Milestone 221 added the tick route: the kernel signals a rendezvous from `sched::on_tick` and one
# worker per group blocks on it through `Irq::WAIT`, which is what makes the run cross cores at all.
# Before it, `crossings` froze inside the first second and stayed frozen; see notes/soak.md.
#
# Exit statuses are `script/board-console`'s, because the judging is literally the same code:
#
#   0  watched the whole duration with the workload beating and nothing announced
#   1  it announced a failure (a soak verdict, or a kernel panic)
#   2  it went quiet, which is what a hang looks like from this end
#   3  QEMU exited before the deadline, or the workload never started
#   4  the build failed, or the arguments were wrong
#
# BUGS
#
# - **A clean run is a number, not a verdict.** The round-trip total exists so that a later run can
#   be compared against it. Nothing here licenses "the concurrency is correct", and the tool says so
#   on every green run for exactly that reason.
# - **QEMU cannot show the defects this is for.** That is the premise of the risk, not a limitation
#   of this script: a green soak under emulation says the mechanism works, and says nothing about
#   silicon.
# - **A soak build is not a production build.** The counters and `Thread::last_cpu` exist only under
#   `--features soak_test`, and they are not free: `ipc_fastpath` measures 1.05 to 1.06x its production
#   size with them in. So these round-trip rates are soak-build rates, comparable with other soak
#   runs and with nothing else. `script/bench` is the instrument for IPC cost. Milestone 221 added
#   nothing to that figure, which was measured rather than assumed (notes/soak.md).
# - **The crossings are tick waiters, not the IPC pairs.** The summary says so on every run that has
#   any, because the flattering reading (that the workload itself is migrating) is available and
#   wrong: rendezvous wakes stay local whatever else is happening. Only a periodic rebalancer would
#   move the pairs, and design/decisions/0138-cross-core-handoff-under-load.md declines one.
# - **`wakes` is not part of `rounds`** and the two rates are not comparable with each other. A wake
#   is not a round trip, and `wakerate` is a property of the machine (TICK_HZ times the online
#   cores) rather than of the workload.
# - **`--arch x86_64` soaks two cores** since 2026-09-23, because that runner's `NIFE_SMP` default
#   moved from 1 to 2 when milestone 315 (a port revoke that reaches every core) closed the last
#   defect holding it there. It soaked one
#   before that, and `remote=0` in the report of such a run is how it says a single-core soak is not
#   a multicore soak. `--smp` still overrides in either direction; three cores and above remain
#   opt-in (`arch::x86_64::ap_boot`'s BUGS #1 is closed on TCG and unmeasured on silicon).
#
# Name: ratified 2026-09-14 (calef, shown `script/soak` for ratification; milestone 297 performed
# the rename). Coined as `soak` by milestone 219's lane on 2026-09-01: a noun, hyphen-free because it
# was one word, and the word the risk's own text uses. `stress` was refused then and stays refused:
# `script/repeat-under-load` already owns that word here, for induced HOST load, which points the
# other way.
#
# **The argument that lost is recorded because it was a real one**, and it is why this was a
# milestone rather than a signature. The maintainer recommended keeping `soak`, on the grounds that
# `soak` alone already names the activity where `stranger` alone names a person, so `stranger-test`
# needs its suffix and this did not. Three costs were named with it: `soak` is the field's term as a
# **noun**; it composes into a sibling cargo feature (`reboot_soak = ["soak", "board"]`) that a
# suffix makes longer; and the rename reaches 36 `cfg` sites plus the console markers and everything
# that reads them. calef ruled the other way, and the ruling accepts that cost.
#
# **The markers moved with the command, and that is a rule rather than a taste.** calef ratified it
# hours earlier the same day: a console marker takes the spelling of the command a reader typed to
# produce it, not of the module that implements it, because the reader's path to the string runs
# through the command. So `kernel/src/soak.rs` prints `soak-test:`, `soak-test-census:` and
# `soak-test-reboot:` where it printed `soak:`, `soak-census:` and `soak-reboot:`.
#
# **What deliberately did NOT move**, because these name the *workload* rather than the command, and
# the workload is still a soak: the crate `soak_page` (ratified 2026-09-13 in its own right), the
# program `soaker`, the kernel module `kernel/src/soak.rs`, `notes/soak.md`, `serial_console`'s
# `Stage::Soak` and its `--until soak` token, `BootProgress::soak`, and `script/board-image --soak`.
# Each of those is calef's own call and none of them was put to him; milestone 297's report proposes
# the sweep as its own piece of work rather than taking it here.

set -e
# A heavy job: wait for one of the machine's heavy-job slots first (helpers/heavy_slot.py).
. "$(dirname "$0")/../helpers/heavy-slot.sh"
cd "$(dirname "$0")/.."

# This project's own QEMU, if `script/ci-qemu` has built one (helpers/qemu-path.sh says why
# PATH is the mechanism). A no-op on macOS and on any machine without that prefix.
. helpers/qemu-path.sh

exec cargo xtask soak-test "$@"
